            <ArticleTitle>Transverse sinus thrombosis presenting with acute hydrocephalus: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>290-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4610.2007.01015.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report on a 32-year-old woman who presented with headache of a 10-day duration, due to acute hydrocephalus. This was a result of a tumefactive lesion of the posterior fossa, which was later proven to be a cerebellar venous infarct caused by unilateral transverse sinus thrombosis. Cerebral dural sinus thrombosis should be considered in the differential diagnosis of new onset of headache.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
